---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import QuickAnswer from "../components/QuickAnswer.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import ProductCard from "../components/ProductCard.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "Best Self-Propelled Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Lawn Mowers for Small Yards",
    "url": "/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Riding Lawn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-riding-lawn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Zero-Turn Mowers",
    "url": "/best-zero-turn-mower/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Weed Eaters",
    "url": "/best-weed-eater/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "6 Best Gas Lawn Mowers (2026): Push and Self-Propelled Ranked";
const description =
  "The Toro 21-Inch Super Recycler and its aluminum deck top 6 gas mowers; the Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace suits a quarter- to half-acre lawn.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "toro-super-recycler-21",
    "name": "Toro 21-Inch Super Recycler Personal Pace",
    "category": "Self-Propelled | Cast Aluminum Deck",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Cast aluminum deck will not rust",
      "Personal Pace drive responds to your walking speed",
      "Excellent mulching from the recycler deck design",
      "Long-lived build suits owners who keep mowers for years",
      "Broad Toro dealer and retail network"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than steel-deck mowers",
      "Heavier than budget push mowers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The gas walk-behind to buy if you want one mower that lasts a decade or more.",
    "description": "This is the pick for owners who plan to keep one mower for the long haul. The deck is cast aluminum instead of stamped steel, so it cannot rust through at the seams the way a neglected steel deck eventually does, and owners commonly keep these machines running long after cheaper mowers have gone for scrap. The recycler deck shape keeps clippings moving so they are cut several times before they fall, which leaves a fine mulch that disappears into the turf. Toro's Personal Pace drive reads how hard you push on the handle and matches your walking speed, with no dial to set. It costs noticeably more than steel-deck rivals and weighs more than a basic push mower, so it is the wrong buy for a small, flat lawn on a tight budget. On a quarter- to half-acre yard, with an owner who means to keep the machine for years, the deck alone justifies the price."
  },
  {
    "id": "toro-recycler-22-gas-selfpropelled",
    "name": "Toro Recycler 22-Inch Personal Pace",
    "category": "Self-Propelled | 22-in. Steel Deck",
    "badge": "Best Mid-Price Self-Propelled",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Personal Pace drive at a mid-range price",
      "22-inch deck covers a bit more ground per pass",
      "Good mulching performance",
      "Folds for storage",
      "Easy to find parts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Steel deck needs cleaning to avoid rust",
      "Front-wheel drive is weaker uphill"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Most of the Super Recycler's ease of use for less money, with a steel deck.",
    "description": "The standard Recycler delivers Toro's Personal Pace drive and a well-shaped mulching deck for a price closer to mainstream self-propelled mowers. Its 22-inch deck is a touch wider than the usual 21 inches, which saves a few passes on a typical suburban yard. Owners commonly call the drive intuitive: push harder and it speeds up, ease off and it slows down. The deck is steel rather than aluminum, so keeping it clean and dry matters for long-term rust resistance, and the front-wheel drive loses grip on steep slopes or with a full bag. On flat-to-rolling lawns up to roughly half an acre, it is one of the easiest gas mowers to own. Step up to the Super Recycler for the long-life deck; stay here if saving money comes first, because this is the better value."
  },
  {
    "id": "troy-bilt-tb230-gas",
    "name": "Troy-Bilt TB230 Self-Propelled",
    "category": "Self-Propelled | Front Drive | High Wheels",
    "badge": "Best Budget Self-Propelled",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Low price for a self-propelled gas mower",
      "Larger rear wheels roll over bumps",
      "3-in-1 bag, mulch or side discharge",
      "Simple single-speed drive",
      "Parts widely available"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Single speed may not suit every walker",
      "Steel deck and lighter build than premium mowers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A plain, affordable self-propelled for flat lawns.",
    "description": "The TB230 is a no-frills self-propelled mower priced below most rivals with powered drive. Its front wheels are driven at a single speed, which suits a steady walker on level ground. Larger rear wheels help it ride over lumpy turf without scalping, and the 3-in-1 deck bags, mulches or side-discharges as the season demands. Owners commonly report dependable starts when it gets fresh fuel and basic care. As with any budget mower, the build is lighter than premium brands, and the steel deck needs regular cleaning to hold off rust. One speed can feel too quick for some people and too slow for others, so try it in person if you can. For a flat lawn up to about a third of an acre, and an owner who wants self-propulsion without paying for extras they will not use, it makes good sense."
  },
  {
    "id": "craftsman-m115",
    "name": "Craftsman M115 21-Inch Push Mower",
    "category": "Push | 3-in-1 | Entry Price",
    "badge": "Best Budget Push",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Among the cheapest gas mowers from a major brand",
      "3-in-1 deck bags, mulches or side-discharges",
      "Light enough to push on small flat lawns",
      "Simple to maintain",
      "Widely stocked parts"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "No self-propel drive",
      "Basic handle and wheels"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "An inexpensive gas push mower for small, flat lawns.",
    "description": "The appeal of the M115 is that it is a plain gas push mower and nothing more. It brings a 21-inch steel deck, a mainstream small engine and the choice of bagging, mulching or side discharge, at one of the lowest prices from a major brand. With no drive system, it weighs less than self-propelled models and has fewer parts to wear, which suits small, flat lawns where pushing is easy. Owners commonly describe it as a reliable starter mower when it gets fresh fuel and a new air filter and spark plug every season or two. Its limits are easy to see: pushing gets tiring on bigger lawns or any slope, and the handle and wheels are basic. On a level lawn under about a quarter acre, it is all the gas mower you need. With slopes, move up to a self-propelled model."
  },
  {
    "id": "troy-bilt-tb110",
    "name": "Troy-Bilt TB110 21-Inch Push Mower",
    "category": "Push | 3-in-1 | High Rear Wheels",
    "badge": "Best Value Push",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Low price with 3-in-1 capability",
      "High rear wheels ease pushing on uneven ground",
      "Simple, reliable design",
      "Easy to store",
      "Wide parts availability"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Push only",
      "Steel deck needs care"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A well-rounded push mower for small lawns at an entry price.",
    "description": "Troy-Bilt's entry push mower stands apart from some rivals thanks to its larger rear wheels. They roll over bumpy turf more easily and make pushing a little less tiring on lawns that are not perfectly smooth. The 21-inch deck bags, mulches and side-discharges, and the design is simple enough for most owners to handle routine maintenance themselves. Owners commonly praise how reliable it is with basic care. It shares every push mower's limits: it is happiest on small, flat lawns, and the steel deck will rust if it is left dirty and damp. Choosing between this and the Craftsman M115 often comes down to price and what your nearest store stocks, and either is a fair pick for a small yard. Keep a spare blade and air filter on the shelf and it stays ready to go."
  },
  {
    "id": "cub-cadet-sc100",
    "name": "Cub Cadet SC 100 21-Inch Push Mower",
    "category": "Push | 3-in-1 | Mid-Price",
    "badge": "Best Cut for a Push Mower",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "pros": [
      "Deck designed for an even, clean cut",
      "3-in-1 capability",
      "Comfortable handle",
      "Solid feel for a push mower",
      "Good dealer support"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Costs more than entry push mowers",
      "Heavier than the cheapest models"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A step up in finish and feel for owners who want a push mower that looks after the lawn.",
    "description": "The SC 100 is priced above entry push mowers and tries to earn the difference with cut quality and a sturdier feel. Cub Cadet shapes the deck to lift grass for an even cut, and owners commonly mention a cleaner finish than cheaper push mowers leave. The handle is comfortable and adjustable, and the 3-in-1 deck covers bagging, mulching and side discharge. It weighs more than the most basic push mowers, which some owners say makes it feel more stable, and Cub Cadet's dealer network helps when it needs service. The real question is whether you need it: on a small, flat lawn, the Craftsman or Troy-Bilt does the job for less. If the finish matters to you and you want a push mower that feels more solid, the SC 100 is a worthwhile step up."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Gas Mower Picks",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-gas-lawn-mowers/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which gas mower should I buy for a typical suburban lawn?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For a lawn between a quarter and half an acre, the Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace is the value pick, with an intuitive drive and good mulching for a fair price. Owners who want a mower to last many years should pay more for the aluminum-deck Toro Super Recycler. Small, flat lawns on tight budgets are well served by the Craftsman M115 or Troy-Bilt TB110 push mowers."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "When is a gas push mower enough?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Up to about a quarter acre of flat lawn, a push mower is all you need, and it is lighter, less expensive and simpler than a self-propelled one. Bigger lawns, slopes and frequent bagging are where a powered drive starts saving real effort. A push mower is also easier to fit into a small garage or shed, and with fewer moving parts it needs fewer repairs."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Gas or battery: which suits my lawn?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Battery mowers fit small to mid-size lawns cut on a regular schedule; they run quietly and need little maintenance. Gas fits large lawns, thick or tall grass and irregular mowing, where runtime and steady power count. Weigh your lawn size, your grass conditions and how much upkeep you will do. Already owning a battery tool platform tips a smaller lawn toward battery."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should a gas mower's oil be changed?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Do the first change after a new engine's first few hours, then change it at least once a season or about every 50 hours, which is what most manufacturers specify. Check the manual for the exact schedule and oil type, and glance at the dipstick before each mow. The job takes roughly fifteen minutes and is the cheapest insurance a small engine has."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the right way to store a gas mower over winter?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Start with the fuel: run the tank empty, or fill it with stabilized fresh gas and let the engine run briefly. Next, change the oil, clean the deck and sharpen the blade, then park the mower somewhere dry and away from ignition sources, taking any starting battery indoors. It all takes less than an hour and prevents most spring starting trouble."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What should I budget for a gas mower?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Expect about $250 to $350 for an entry push mower, roughly $350 to $700 for a self-propelled one and $700 or more for a premium aluminum-deck model, at the time of writing. Yearly oil, filters, plugs and stabilizer add a few tens of dollars. A better deck costs a bit more at the start and usually repays it through extra years of use."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are Honda gas mowers still available?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mostly as leftover stock. Honda has wound down production of residential walk-behind mowers for the US market, and the remaining HRX and HRR models are largely dealer inventory. They are still good mowers and parts support should continue, but do not count on finding one new for much longer. If you buy one, register it so any service notices reach you."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Lawn Mowers & Tractors",
    tags: [
      "best gas lawn mowers",
      "gas push mower",
      "gas self-propelled mower",
      "lawn mower engine",
      "mower winter storage",
      "lawn mower maintenance"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <header data-hero class="bg-[#F4F6F8] border-b border-gray-200">
      <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-10 pb-8 lg:pt-14 lg:pb-10">
        <p class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-2 gap-y-1 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest m-0 mb-4">
          <span class="text-[#D93A00]">Buyer's Guide</span>
          <span class="text-gray-300" aria-hidden="true">&bull;</span>
          <span class="text-gray-500">Updated {dates.display}</span>
        </p>
        <h1 class="text-[2.1rem] leading-[1.15] sm:text-5xl font-black tracking-tight text-gray-900 m-0">
          The Best Gas Lawn Mowers
        </h1>
        <QuickAnswer products={PRODUCTS} />
        <p class="text-lg lg:text-xl text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-5 mb-0">
          The Toro 21-Inch Super Recycler is the best gas lawn mower for most buyers, because its cast aluminum deck will not rust. Lawn size, slopes and budget change the pick: a push mower covers a flat lawn under a quarter acre, and steel-deck models cost less. Below are six picks, plus drive, deck, fuel, upkeep and cost answers.
        </p>
        <!-- Author & Meta -->
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3 mt-7">
          <AuthorAvatar size={40} />
          <div class="min-w-0">
            <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 m-0">Written by <a href="/authors/alex-rivers/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 no-underline hover:underline">Alex Rivers</a></p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 m-0">Home Improvement Editor &middot; Updated {dates.display}</p>
          </div>
          <SocialProof users="" />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center gap-4 mt-6">
          <TrustBadge />
          <span class="flex-1 h-px bg-gray-300"></span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-sm text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mt-3 mb-0">
          Based on manufacturer specs for drive, deck, engine and warranty, parts availability and owner feedback. Not hands-on tested. No paid placements. <a href="/how-we-test/" class="font-bold text-gray-900 underline">Read how we pick</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-8">
      <details open class="group bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl px-5 pt-4 pb-2">
        <summary class="flex items-center gap-3 pb-3 cursor-pointer list-none [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 text-gray-500" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" d="M9 6h11M9 12h11M9 18h11M4.5 6h.01M4.5 12h.01M4.5 18h.01" /></svg>
          <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">In This Guide</span>
          <span class="text-xs font-semibold text-gray-400">18 sections</span>
          <svg class="w-5 h-5 ml-auto text-gray-500 transition-transform group-open:rotate-180" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M6 15l6-6 6 6" /></svg>
        </summary>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-x-6">
              <a href="#short-answer" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">1.</span><span>The Short Answer</span></a>
              <a href="#top-picks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">2.</span><span>Which Mower to Buy</span></a>
              <a href="#gas-or-battery" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">3.</span><span>Gas or Battery?</span></a>
              <a href="#push-or-drive" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">4.</span><span>Push or Self-Propelled?</span></a>
              <a href="#engines" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">5.</span><span>What Engine Size?</span></a>
              <a href="#decks" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">6.</span><span>What Deck Size and Material?</span></a>
              <a href="#fuel" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">7.</span><span>Which Gas to Use?</span></a>
              <a href="#clippings" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">8.</span><span>Bag, Mulch or Discharge?</span></a>
              <a href="#oil" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">9.</span><span>How Often to Change Oil?</span></a>
              <a href="#plug-filter" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">10.</span><span>Plug and Air Filter</span></a>
              <a href="#blades" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">11.</span><span>Sharpening the Blade</span></a>
              <a href="#winter" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">12.</span><span>How to Winterize</span></a>
              <a href="#troubleshooting" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">13.</span><span>Why It Won't Start</span></a>
              <a href="#rules" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">14.</span><span>Are Gas Mowers Banned?</span></a>
              <a href="#cost" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">15.</span><span>Cost to Buy and Run</span></a>
              <a href="#safety" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">16.</span><span>Using It Safely</span></a>
              <a href="#mistakes" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">17.</span><span>Common Mistakes</span></a>
              <a href="#faq" class="flex items-baseline gap-3 py-2.5 border-t border-gray-100 text-[15px] text-gray-700 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline"><span class="w-6 shrink-0 text-gray-400 font-semibold">18.</span><span>FAQ</span></a>
        </nav>
      </details>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Gas is no longer the default answer for every yard. On a small lawn that gets cut every week, a decent battery mower runs quieter and asks far less of its owner. A gas engine justifies itself on big lawns, on dense or frequently overgrown grass, and for anyone who wants to finish the whole job on one tank instead of watching a charge gauge. If that sounds like your yard, three decisions remain: push or self-propelled, which deck, and whether you will give the engine about twenty minutes of care each season.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Best Gas Lawn Mowers: The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Buy the <strong>Toro 21-Inch Super Recycler Personal Pace</strong> if you want one gas mower to last a decade or more; its cast aluminum deck cannot rust. The <strong>Toro Recycler 22-Inch</strong> gives most of that ease for less money, and the <strong>Troy-Bilt TB230</strong> is the low-cost self-propelled choice for flat ground. On a level lawn under about a quarter acre, a push mower is enough: the <strong>Craftsman M115</strong> or <strong>Troy-Bilt TB110</strong> on a budget, or the <strong>Cub Cadet SC 100</strong> for a cleaner cut.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Gas mower</th>
