        <div id="gas-or-battery" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Is a Gas Mower Still Worth It Over Battery?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Only for some lawns. Battery mowers have improved enough that the honest first question is whether you need gas at all. On a lawn under roughly a quarter acre that gets cut on schedule, a current battery model starts at the press of a button, stays quiet and needs very little maintenance, and plenty of people who switch never miss the pull cord or the fuel can.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Where gas still wins</h3>

          <p>
            Three situations still favor a gas engine. Large lawns come first: one tank keeps running where a battery mower would need a second pack. Thick, tall or wet grass is the second, because a gas engine holds its power while battery runtime falls off steeply. Irregular mowing is the third; if the grass often gets ahead of you, gas copes with the overgrowth with less fuss.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How the costs compare</h3>

          <p>
            Gas often wins slightly on purchase price at the low end, because a basic gas push mower tends to undercut a similar battery kit. That edge fades over the years: the gas mower needs oil, plugs, filters and stabilizer every season, and the battery mower may need a replacement pack at some point.
          </p>

          <p>
            For compact lawns, our guide to the <a href="/best-lawn-mower-for-small-yard/">best lawn mower for a small yard</a> covers battery and corded models, and the <a href="/best-self-propelled-lawn-mower/">best self-propelled lawn mower</a> guide puts gas and battery drive systems side by side. If your lawn fits the gas profile, keep reading.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="push-or-drive" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Should You Get a Push or Self-Propelled Gas Mower?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Get a push mower for a small, flat lawn and a self-propelled one for anything past about a quarter acre or with a noticeable slope. A push mower has no drive system; the engine turns the blade and you provide the forward motion. That keeps it lighter, cheaper and simpler, with fewer parts to wear out, which is why it is often the smarter purchase on a small level lawn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Adding a transmission, which drives either the front pair of wheels or the rear pair, is what makes a mower self-propelled. You pay for it in price and weight, but in return the machine does most of the work on big lawns, on slopes and while the bag fills. Past roughly a quarter acre, or on ground with a real tilt, that drive pays for itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Front-wheel or rear-wheel drive?</h3>

          <p>
            Front-wheel drive is the cheaper of the two and turns easily, but it loses grip going uphill and when the bag fills. Rear-wheel drive holds traction better on slopes and while bagging. For a hilly lawn, choose rear-wheel drive.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Who will be behind the handle?</h3>

          <p>
            Think about the person doing the mowing, too. A lighter push mower can be easier for some people to steer and to store, while a powered drive helps anyone who finds shoving a heavy machine tiring. If you can, check the handle height and the controls in the store before you buy.
          </p>

