        <div id="safety" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            16. How Do You Use a Gas Lawn Mower Safely?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Fuel it outdoors and cold, run it only in the open air, clear the lawn before you start, and keep hands and feet away from the deck. Each of those habits gets its own answer below.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you refuel?</h3>

          <p>
            Fill the tank outside with the engine off and cool, because fuel spilled on a hot engine can ignite. Wipe up any spill and move the mower away from the spot where you fueled it before starting.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why never run it indoors?</h3>

          <p>
            Small engines produce carbon monoxide, which has no smell and can build to dangerous levels inside a closed garage or shed. Never run a gas mower in either one.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">What should you do before and while mowing?</h3>

          <p>
            Walk the lawn first and pick up sticks, stones and toys, and keep children and pets inside while you mow. Dress for it: sturdy shoes, long pants, and protection for your eyes and ears. Keep your hands out from under the deck whenever the engine is running.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">How should you handle slopes, wet grass and edges?</h3>

          <p>
            With a walk-behind, mow across the face of a slope rather than up and down it, so the mower cannot roll back onto your feet. Skip wet grass, which causes slips. Finish the edges with a <a href="/best-weed-eater/">string trimmer</a> instead of tipping the mower to reach them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            17. Common Mistakes With Gas Lawn Mowers
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving fuel in the tank over winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Stale fuel causes more spring no-starts than anything else. When the season ends, burn the tank dry or fill it with stabilized fresh gas and let the engine run until the treated fuel has reached the carburetor. While mowing season lasts, buy fuel in small batches you will use up within about a month.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a push mower for a sloped or large lawn</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shoving a push mower up slopes or across a big lawn every week wears you out. Past about a quarter acre, or on any noticeable slope, a self-propelled model is worth the extra money, ideally with rear-wheel drive where there are hills. Your back and your weekends both come out ahead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring oil changes</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Small engines live on clean oil. Check the dipstick before each mow and change the oil at least once a season. Running low, or running on dirty oil, shortens engine life and can cause a sudden failure. Keep a spare quart on the shelf so a top-up is never the reason you skip it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Mowing with a dull blade</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A dull blade rips grass instead of cutting it, and the torn tips turn brown within a day and invite disease. Sharpen at least once a season, keep the blade balanced, and replace it once it is bent or worn thin. Having a spare blade ready makes the swap quick.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the mower in an enclosed space</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Gas engines produce carbon monoxide, which builds fast in a closed space and gives no warning smell. Never start or run a mower inside a shut garage or shed. Start it outdoors, keep the door open and the engine outside, and let it cool before you wheel it back in.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

