        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            18. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which gas mower should I buy for a typical suburban lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For a lawn between a quarter and half an acre, the Toro Recycler 22-inch Personal Pace is the value pick, with an intuitive drive and good mulching for a fair price. Owners who want a mower to last many years should pay more for the aluminum-deck Toro Super Recycler. Small, flat lawns on tight budgets are well served by the Craftsman M115 or Troy-Bilt TB110 push mowers.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is a gas push mower enough?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Up to about a quarter acre of flat lawn, a push mower is all you need, and it is lighter, less expensive and simpler than a self-propelled one. Bigger lawns, slopes and frequent bagging are where a powered drive starts saving real effort. A push mower is also easier to fit into a small garage or shed, and with fewer moving parts it needs fewer repairs.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Gas or battery: which suits my lawn?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Battery mowers fit small to mid-size lawns cut on a regular schedule; they run quietly and need little maintenance. Gas fits large lawns, thick or tall grass and irregular mowing, where runtime and steady power count. Weigh your lawn size, your grass conditions and how much upkeep you will do. Already owning a battery tool platform tips a smaller lawn toward battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should a gas mower's oil be changed?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Do the first change after a new engine's first few hours, then change it at least once a season or about every 50 hours, which is what most manufacturers specify. Check the manual for the exact schedule and oil type, and glance at the dipstick before each mow. The job takes roughly fifteen minutes and is the cheapest insurance a small engine has.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the right way to store a gas mower over winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Start with the fuel: run the tank empty, or fill it with stabilized fresh gas and let the engine run briefly. Next, change the oil, clean the deck and sharpen the blade, then park the mower somewhere dry and away from ignition sources, taking any starting battery indoors. It all takes less than an hour and prevents most spring starting trouble.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What should I budget for a gas mower?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Expect about $250 to $350 for an entry push mower, roughly $350 to $700 for a self-propelled one and $700 or more for a premium aluminum-deck model, at the time of writing. Yearly oil, filters, plugs and stabilizer add a few tens of dollars. A better deck costs a bit more at the start and usually repays it through extra years of use.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are Honda gas mowers still available?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Mostly as leftover stock. Honda has wound down production of residential walk-behind mowers for the US market, and the remaining HRX and HRR models are largely dealer inventory. They are still good mowers and parts support should continue, but do not count on finding one new for much longer. If you buy one, register it so any service notices reach you.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
